{-
   The number and the name a payload-free sum is - Analysis-Language.md §5.1.

   One class, and a file of its own because it is the one class in this module that nothing in this
   module can join. Every instance of it is generated: `deriving` is not written, an author cannot
   write one, and what it answers is decided by the `data` declaration and by nothing else. The
   classes in `Core.yana` beside it are all the other kind - the ones a user type joins by writing
   an instance - so keeping this one with them said that it was the same sort of thing.
-}

{-
   The number a payload-free sum is - Analysis-Language.md §5.1.

   A `data` whose constructors all carry nothing already lowers to an integer, and `@value(n)` is how
   a declaration says *which* integer: `data Whence = @value(0) FromStart | @value(1) FromCurrent |
   @value(2) FromEnd` is the three numbers `lseek` takes, said once, where the alternative was a
   nineteen-line `match` per boundary crossing.

   **Nobody writes an instance of this.** It is generated for every payload-free sum, pinned or not,
   because what all four functions do is decided by the declaration and there is nothing an author
   could add. Writing one is refused for the same reason writing `instance Num(Int)` is.

   `valueOf` is total and free - the value *is* the number, so it compiles to a reinterpretation and
   the optimizer sees straight through it. `fromValue` is partial and says so: a number the
   declaration does not name is a number that is not one of these, and `Maybe` is where that goes.
   Its cost is a membership test - two comparisons where the values run consecutively, and one per
   constructor where they do not, which is what a set like `errno`'s is.
-}
pub class Enum(a):
  fn valueOf(item: a) -> I64
  fn fromValue(number: I64) -> Maybe(a)

  -- The same pair over the constructor's *name*, which is the other thing a declaration decides and
  -- the compiler is the only thing that knows.
  --
  -- Here rather than in a class of its own because it is the same statement in a second currency: a
  -- payload-free sum is a closed set of alternatives, and `@value(n)` says which number each one is
  -- while the declaration itself says which word each one is. There is nothing an author could
  -- contribute to either, so both are generated and neither may be written.
  --
  -- `nameOf` is total and its result is a literal: a chain of comparisons picking one constant
  -- string, which folds to that string outright wherever the constructor is known. `fromName` is
  -- partial for the reason `fromValue` is - a word the declaration does not name is not one of these
  -- - and it is the one function here that is not free, being a string comparison per candidate. Its
  -- generated form compares lengths first and only then contents, so a name of the wrong length
  -- costs one integer compare however many constructors the type has.
  --
  -- These are the derived `Show` for an enum, made available to a program rather than only to the
  -- formatter: `describeError` and `nameOf` answer different questions about one value, and a type
  -- that wants prose keeps writing the first while getting the second for nothing.
  fn nameOf(item: a) -> String
  fn fromName(name: String) -> Maybe(a)
